 ## Background
-The method was developed as part of parallel volume visualization techniques for time-varying data [S2, unofficial]. It is based on the principle of spatial division of uniform resolution data, where the volume is partitioned along one axis to allow independent rendering by multiple processors [S2, unofficial]. The sewing step for isosurface extraction was introduced to handle the concatenation of partial meshes generated by parallel processes [S1].
+The method is described in the context of parallel volume visualization for time-varying datasets, specifically handling spatial division of a single time step [S2, unofficial]. It is based on the principle of spatial division of uniform resolution data, where the volume is partitioned along one axis to allow independent rendering by multiple processors [S2, unofficial]. The sewing step for isosurface extraction was introduced to handle the concatenation of partial meshes generated by parallel processes [S1].
 
